# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Title repeated + Introduction
H1("Governing Agentic AI Coding Assistants in the Software Industry", page_break=True)
H1("Introduction")
P("Software teams are giving artificial intelligence (AI) agents real authority. Coding assistants that once offered single-line completions now plan multistep changes, call external tools, and act on a developer's machine or a build server. When such an agent misreads its instructions, follows a hidden command in a repository file, or runs with broader permissions than intended, the result is not just a bug. It is a lapse in accountability, consent, and control. This report shows how often those lapses occur in disclosed weaknesses, which governance controls they involve, whether control failures go with more serious weaknesses, and whether a large language model (LLM) can apply the governance rulebook as reliably as people can.")
H2("Background and Context")
P("The setting is the software industry, where coding agents such as Claude Code, Cursor, GitHub Copilot, OpenAI Codex, Gemini command-line interface (CLI), Cline, and Kiro read whole repositories, run terminal commands, and connect to tools through the Model Context Protocol (MCP; Hou et al., 2025). Benchmarks such as SWE-bench show that agents built on transformer language models can now resolve real GitHub issues end to end (Jimenez et al., 2024; Vaswani et al., 2017), and interface designs such as SWE-agent widen what they can do (Yang et al., 2024). The stakeholders are engineering leaders and platform teams who choose and configure agents, security and AI-governance functions who approve them, risk committees who answer for incidents, and vendors who build the tools. In 2025, a Replit agent deleted a production database during a declared code freeze (Responsible AI Collaborative, 2025). The case showed that failures of agent autonomy are operational and reputational events, not hypothetical ones.")
P("Governance guidance exists but is hard to apply to coding agents. The AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF) of the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) defines govern, map, measure, and manage functions (NIST, 2023). The International Organization for Standardization and the International Electrotechnical Commission (ISO/IEC) standard 42001 sets requirements for an AI management system (ISO/IEC, 2023). The Open Worldwide Application Security Project (OWASP) Top 10 for Agentic Applications names the leading agent risks (OWASP Gen AI Security Project, 2025). None of these instruments gives a compact, testable control set for coding agents, and none has been checked against field evidence. Research on agent governance argues that autonomy creates accountability gaps and recommends practices such as approval gates and attributable actions (Chan et al., 2023; Shavit et al., 2023), but it does not measure how often those practices fail in shipped products.")
P("Field evidence does exist. When a coding agent has a security weakness, the vendor or a vulnerability numbering authority publishes an advisory with a Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) identifier, a Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) type, and a severity score from the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS; Forum of Incident Response and Security Teams [FIRST], 2023). Disclosures have grown quickly: 155 of the 223 eligible advisories studied here were published in 2026 alone. This makes it possible to treat agent governance as a data analytics problem, scoring each disclosed weakness against an explicit control framework and analyzing the body of evidence as a whole instead of one advisory at a time.")
H2("Problem Statement")
P("Software organizations adopt AI coding agents without a measurable, evidence-based way to judge which ethics and governance controls those agents breach, how serious the resulting weaknesses are, or whether compliance can be assessed reliably at scale. The objective of this study is to *predict and explain* Y = whether a disclosed coding-agent weakness is high-impact (CVSS High or Critical, 1/0) for each public security advisory (the unit of analysis) using X = the governance profile of the case under the Agentic Coding Ethics and Governance (ACEG) Framework (the Governance Compliance Score [GCS], the eight governance domains, and the 20 control-breach indicators, with component type, number of domains, publication year, and CWE group as covariates) over advisories published from January 1, 2023, to the data freeze of October 11, 2026. Success is evaluated with the hold-out and cross-validated area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve (AUC), balanced accuracy, F1, and Brier score for prediction; with Wilson 95% confidence intervals (CIs), chi-square tests, and odds ratios (ORs) at α = .05 for the hypotheses; and with Cohen's κ against human consensus (target κ > .60) for automated coding.")
H2("Purpose of the Study")
P("The study follows design-science logic: it builds an artifact, applies it to real data, evaluates it, and refines it (Hevner et al., 2004). Its purpose has four parts. It *estimates* how much of the disclosed evidence the ACEG Framework covers and how common each governance domain is. It *tests an association* between the family of the main governance failure and the need for human interaction during exploitation. It *classifies and explains* high-impact cases with logistic regression, tree ensembles, and a neural network, and identifies which breached controls carry the predictive signal. Finally, it *automates and compares*: an LLM coder applies the same codebook as the human coders, and its labels are measured against theirs. The deliverables are a coded public data set, answers to four research questions, an industry-ready ACEG Governance Skill with a scorecard service, and an evidence-based ACEG Version 2.")
H2("Research Problems / Research Questions")
P("**Research Question 1 (RQ1):** What proportion of disclosed coding-agent weaknesses does the ACEG Framework cover, and how prevalent is each of its eight governance domains?")
P("**Research Question 2 (RQ2):** Is the domain family of a case's primary governance failure associated with whether exploiting the weakness requires human interaction?")
P("**Research Question 3 (RQ3):** Does a lower GCS predict a high-impact case after adjusting for component type, number of domains, and publication year, and which model and features predict high impact best?")
P("**Research Question 4 (RQ4):** How closely does an automated LLM coder reproduce human coding when both apply the same ACEG codebook?")
H2("Contributions and Expected Value")
P("*Practical contribution.* The study gives software organizations a ready-to-use control framework: eight domains, 20 controls, and an OWASP and NIST crosswalk. The ACEG Governance Skill, a portable agent skill that any LLM agent platform can load, turns a newly disclosed agent weakness into governance codes, a governance score, a predicted probability of high impact, a priority tier, and a remediation plan with owners and verification checks.")
P("*Technical contribution.* The study contributes a coded census of 223 coding-agent advisories, a reproducible SQLite pipeline, a head-to-head comparison of eight predictive models (logistic regression, random forest, gradient boosting, and a multilayer perceptron [MLP] neural network) under a hold-out split and repeated cross-validation, and a three-coder reliability design in which an LLM coder is scored only on cases where the two humans already agree.")
P("*Value to stakeholders.* Platform and DevSecOps teams get triage priorities grounded in field evidence instead of vendor claims. AI-governance offices and auditors working to ISO/IEC 42001 get controls they can test against artefacts. Agent vendors get a domain-level view of where their peers' products fail. For the research community, the human-versus-LLM results show where AI-assisted governance coding can be trusted and where human review must remain.")

