P("*Security of what assistants produce.* The first body of work examines the code that assistants write. Pearce et al. (2022) found weaknesses in roughly two of every five Copilot-generated programs, and Perry et al. (2023) showed in a controlled experiment that developers using an assistant wrote less secure code while feeling more confident about it. These studies examine outputs, not actions. They do not cover an agent that runs a command, edits a configuration file, or calls a tool on its own initiative. That gap explains why ACEG gives four of its eight domains to agency, boundaries, oversight, and supply-chain integrity rather than to code quality, and why the perceived safety of a human checkpoint is tested in RQ2 rather than assumed.")
P("*Attacks on agents and the tool ecosystem.* A second body of work shows that the inputs an agent reads can take over its goals. Greshake et al. (2023) described indirect prompt injection, in which instructions hidden in retrieved content redirect an LLM application without any action by the user. InjecAgent and AgentDojo then measured how widely tool-using agents fall for such attacks and how little current defenses help (Debenedetti et al., 2024; Zhan et al., 2024). Hou et al. (2025) extended the threat model to MCP servers, which are now a standard way to give coding agents new tools. Benchmarks control the task, but they cannot show how often each weakness reaches shipped products. The advisory census in this study supplies that field prevalence (RQ1), and component type enters RQ3 as a covariate because many advisories concern extensions and servers rather than the agents themselves.")
P("*Governance without measurement.* Chan et al. (2023) argued that agency, meaning goal-directedness, autonomy, and long-horizon action, spreads responsibility thinly and raises systemic harms. Shavit et al. (2023) proposed concrete practices such as constraining the action space, requiring approval, keeping actions legible, and making them attributable and interruptible. The NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001, and the OWASP agentic list turn similar ideas into functions and risk catalogues. What none of these sources provides is evidence that compliance with the controls relates to the seriousness of real failures. ACEG combines them into eight domains and 20 controls and adds the GCS, so the link between compliance and severity becomes a testable hypothesis (RQ3).")
P("*Evidence quality and automated coding.* Incident catalogues such as the AI Incident Database show the value of collecting failures but record them unevenly (McGregor, 2021). Security advisories are more standardized and carry a CVSS rating. That rating measures technical impact rather than exploitation risk (Allodi & Massacci, 2014), so this study treats CVSS as an imperfect outcome and audits it. Coding hundreds of advisories by hand is slow, which is why recent work on LLM annotators matters. Gilardi et al. (2023) found zero-shot ChatGPT more accurate and more consistent than crowd workers on text-classification tasks. Zheng et al. (2023) reported that strong LLM judges agree with humans about as often as humans agree with each other, and Cemri et al. (2025) used an LLM annotator to apply a failure taxonomy to agent traces. These agentic and LLM studies shaped RQ4. The LLM coder receives the codebook verbatim and never sees severity, and it is judged against human agreement with a chance-corrected statistic (Cohen, 1960; Landis & Koch, 1977).")
P("*Model choice for small tabular data.* Deep learning (DL) learns representations through many layers of nonlinear transformation (LeCun et al., 2015) and underlies both the transformer models that power coding agents (Vaswani et al., 2017) and the LLM coder used here. For small structured data sets like this one, however, Grinsztajn et al. (2022) showed across 45 benchmarks that tree ensembles such as random forests (Breiman, 2001) and gradient boosting (Friedman, 2001) usually beat deep networks. The study therefore treats tree ensembles as its main learners, includes an MLP trained by backpropagation (Rumelhart et al., 1986) as the DL comparator, and keeps logistic regression as the interpretable model for the hypothesis test (Hosmer et al., 2013).")
P("*Gap addressed.* Taken together, the literature describes coding-agent risks in the laboratory and recommends governance practices in principle. It does not score disclosed field weaknesses against an explicit control framework, test whether control failures predict severity, or check whether an LLM can apply such a framework reliably. This study addresses all three gaps.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Materials and Method
H1("Materials and Method")
H2("Data Source and Acquisition")
P("The study uses two public sources: the GitHub Advisory Database (GitHub, 2026; https://github.com/github/advisory-database; Creative Commons Attribution 4.0) and the CVE List V5 maintained by the CVE Program (CVE Program, 2026; https://github.com/CVEProject/cvelistV5). Both are Git repositories of JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) records. They were acquired by cloning them at commits dated October 11, 2026, and the commit hashes are recorded in docs/data_freeze.md, so no web scraping, account, or application programming interface (API) key was needed. The script src/01_collect.py searched both clones for records published from January 1, 2023, that name a coding agent or one of its components (for example, Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot agent features, Codex CLI, Cline, Roo Code, Kiro, Aider, OpenHands, or MCP servers built for these agents). It found 239 candidate records. The script src/02_screen.py merged records that appear in both sources on their CVE identifier and applied the eligibility rules.")
H2("Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria")
P("A record was *included* if it disclosed a weakness in (a) an AI coding agent able to read, write, or execute code, commands, or tool calls, or (b) a component such an agent runs or that wraps it, such as an extension, plugin, hook, MCP server, bridge, build workflow, or distribution pipeline; it also had to be published between January 1, 2023, and October 11, 2026. A record was *excluded* for one of three reasons: E1, the product is not a coding agent or one of its components (14 records, for example browser assistants and name collisions); E2, the product only offers autocompletion with no ability to act (one record); or E3, the record has too little detail to code any domain (one record). The screening followed the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) flow (Page et al., 2021): 239 identified, 16 excluded, and 223 included. Because these are all the eligible records in the period, the study analyzes a census rather than a sample.")
H2("Data Description and Data Dictionary")
P(f"The analysis file has one row per vulnerability: 223 cases and 21 source variables, to which coding adds the domain, control, and score fields. Cases run from October 31, 2023, to October 10, 2026. Of these, 155 were published in 2026, 66 in 2025, and one each in 2023 and 2024. The cases cover 20 products: 176 records concern agent products and 47 concern ecosystem components. The dependent variable, high_impact, equals 1 when the published CVSS severity is High or Critical (base score ≥ 7.0). That applies to 156 cases (70.0%): 126 High, 30 Critical, 49 Medium, and 18 Low. Table A1 in Appendix A is the full data dictionary, giving each variable's definition, type, allowed values, missing-value treatment, and analytic role.")
H2("Database Setup and Data Access")
P("All analytic data live in one SQLite database (data/aceg.db; SQLite 3.53), created by src/08_build_gold_and_db.py. SQLite was chosen because it is a serverless, single-file relational store that any reviewer can open without installing a server, while still enforcing a schema and supporting Structured Query Language (SQL) joins and views. The database holds six tables and two views. The *cases* table holds the 223 source records, with a unique index on case_id. The tables *codes_coder1*, *codes_coder2*, *codes_llm*, and *codes_gold* hold one row per case for each label source, each with the same 30 coded fields and the derived scores. The *cleaning_log* table records every correction made to the coders' sheets. The view *v_analysis* joins the cases to the final labels and is the only input to the analysis. The view *v_agreement* lines up the three coders for reliability checks. The analysis script reads the data with pandas through Python's sqlite3 driver, for example SELECT * FROM v_analysis ORDER BY case_id, so every result traces back to a documented query. The completed coding workbooks are kept unchanged in coding/completed/ as the system of record.")
H2("Coding Procedure and Label Construction")
P("Every case was coded against ACEG Version 1 (Appendix B). Coding a case means marking each of the eight domains present or absent, choosing one primary domain, coding each control of a present domain as breached (1) or not breached (0), and judging whether a breached control would have prevented the weakness (covered). Two human coders worked independently in separate copies of the coding workbook. Their worksheets showed only the case identifier, product, title, description, and link. Severity fields were withheld so that control judgments could not be influenced by the outcome.")
P("The automated coder was Claude Opus 5.5, Anthropic's LLM, acting as an agentic annotator. It received the codebook rules and each case's title and description, with any severity wording masked by a regular expression (data/llm/blinded_case_text.txt). It returned a compact record per case: domains present, primary domain, breached controls, and coverage. Controls of present domains that were not listed as breached were coded 0, as the codebook requires. The LLM coder never saw the human labels, the CVSS fields, or the outcome. The coding was done in one interactive session, not through the batch API script (src/03_llm_code.py), and Appendix F describes the ACEG Governance Skill built from the same coding procedure.")
P(f"Two cleaning rules were applied to the human sheets before the labels were combined. First, Coder 1 had entered values for {len(pd.read_csv(T / 'cleaning_log_coding.csv'))} control cells that belonged to domains the same coder had marked absent; under codebook rule 4 these were reset to not applicable, and each change is logged. Second, all values were normalized to 1, 0, or not applicable. Final labels were then formed cell by cell as the majority of the three coders. A domain is present when at least two coders coded it present, and a control is breached when most of the coders who judged it coded it breached. Seven cases in which no domain reached two votes, and 51 primary-domain ties, were resolved with pre-stated tie-break rules: most votes first, then the earliest point in the causal chain. The rules are documented in the script header. Because the final labels include the LLM's vote, RQ4 does not use them. It scores the LLM only against cases on which the two humans agreed.")
H2("Exploratory Data Analysis")
P("The exploratory data analysis (EDA) asked four questions that shaped the models: how the cases are spread over time, how the outcome is distributed, whether cases cluster by product, and which characteristics are associated with high impact. Figure 1 shows that disclosures rose from a handful a quarter before mid-2025 to 64 in the third quarter of 2026, while the High and Critical share stayed high throughout. The outcome is therefore not an artefact of one period, and because 2023 and 2024 have only one case each, time enters the models as a single year_2026 indicator.")
FIGURE(1, "Coding-Agent Advisories by Quarter of Publication and CVSS Severity", FIG / "fig_eda_quarterly.png", 6.0,
       "Quarters from 2025 onward are shown. The two earlier cases (2023 and 2024) and the partial fourth quarter of 2026 (data frozen October 11, 2026) are included in all analyses.")
P(f"Base scores cluster between 7.0 and 9.0 (median {S['eda']['cvss_median']:.1f} before recovery; Figure C1). The 70:30 class split means a model that always predicts high impact is already about 70% accurate, so models are judged on AUC, balanced accuracy, and the Brier score (Brier, 1950) rather than raw accuracy. Three products, Claude Code (58 cases), Cursor (32), and GitHub Copilot (24), account for 51% of the cases, so the RQ3 logistic model is re-estimated with standard errors (SEs) clustered by product. Table 2 tests each characteristic against the outcome. Weakness type is strongly associated with high impact (Cramér's V = {assoc.cramers_v[0]:.2f}). Figure 2 shows that every code-injection case and 88% of command-injection cases are high-impact, compared with 29% of information-exposure cases. Component type, publication year, and user interaction show no association. The CVSS version is associated with the outcome, so it is tested as a sensitivity covariate. The attack vector is never used as a predictor because it is part of the CVSS formula.")
