TABLE(4, "Minimum Sample Size by Research Question", ["RQ", "Formula", "Parameters and computation", "Minimum n"], SS, [0.45, 2.1, 3.1, 0.75], size=9,
      notes="e = margin of error; w = Cohen's effect size; λ = noncentrality parameter; P = proportion of high-impact cases (156 of 223); β* = |log odds ratio| per SD = 0.50 (OR ≈ 0.61); a = expected agreement. Reproduced by sample_size/sample_size.py.")
P(f"The binding requirement is max(196, 175, 187, 171) = 196 cases. Only 223 eligible advisories exist, so all of them are analyzed and the collected volume exceeds the minimum by 14%. At N = 223 the RQ1 margin of error narrows to ±6.6 points. RQ2 has power {R2['power_w25']:.2f} to detect w = 0.25 with the {R2['n']} cases that have a CVSS vector. The 67 low-impact cases give 16.8 events per predictor in the four-predictor RQ3 model, above the minimum of 10 (Peduzzi et al., 1996). For RQ4, the per-domain consensus sets contain 94 to 189 cases and the pooled set 1,214 domain cells, above the 171 required. The data volume is therefore sufficient for every research question, and the remaining limits are the specific cells reported with each result, not the overall sample.")
H2("Statistical Methods and Model Selection")
P("Each method was chosen to fit the type of question. RQ1 estimates proportions, so it uses Wilson score intervals, which keep their nominal coverage near 0 and 1 (Wilson, 1927), and an exact binomial test. RQ2 crosses two categorical variables, so it uses Pearson's χ² test with Cramér's V (Cramér, 1946) and standardized residuals. Because some expected counts are below 5, a 10,000-draw Monte Carlo permutation test and a test with merged families are added. RQ3 has a binary outcome, so the hypothesis is tested with binary logistic regression: logit(P[high impact]) = β_{0} + β_{1}GCS_{z} + β_{2}ecosystem + β_{3}n_domains + β_{4}year_2026. Logistic regression gives interpretable odds ratios and has well-understood diagnostics (Hosmer et al., 2013). Its assumptions were checked with the Hosmer–Lemeshow test, the Box–Tidwell test for a linear logit (Box & Tidwell, 1962), variance inflation factors (VIFs), and SEs clustered by product.")
P("Prediction is a separate goal from testing the hypothesis, so eight models were compared. M0 is a majority-class baseline. M1, M2, and M3 are logistic regressions on covariates only, on GCS plus covariates, and on CWE group, the last representing current practice, in which teams triage by weakness type. M4, M5, and M6 are a random forest (Breiman, 2001), histogram gradient boosting (Friedman, 2001), and an MLP neural network, all trained on the full ACEG profile: eight domains, 20 breach indicators, GCS, and covariates. M7 is a random forest on the ACEG profile plus CWE group. Tree ensembles were favoured because they capture interactions between controls without specification and perform best on small tabular data (Grinsztajn et al., 2022). The MLP was included as the DL comparator. RQ4 compares labels from different coders, so it uses Cohen's κ for pairs (Cohen, 1960), Fleiss' κ for three raters (Fleiss, 1971), and accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 for the LLM against the human consensus, with bootstrap CIs (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993). Agreement is read on the Landis and Koch (1977) scale, so κ > .60 means substantial agreement (McHugh, 2012).")
H2("Data Splitting and Validation")
P(f"For prediction, the 223 cases were split once into a stratified training set of {S['split']['train']} cases (75%; {S['split']['train_hi']} high-impact) and a test set of {S['split']['test']} cases (25%; {S['split']['test_hi']} high-impact), using random seed 42. Stratification kept the 70:30 balance in both parts. The case-level split is saved in outputs/final/train_test_split.csv. All model selection used the training set only. Hyperparameters for M4 to M7 were tuned by grid search with stratified five-fold cross-validation (CV) on AUC, and each tuned model was then scored by stratified five-fold cross-validation repeated 10 times (50 folds) on the training set. The best model was chosen *before* the test set was used, by the highest mean cross-validated AUC. The test set was used once, to report out-of-sample performance. As a robustness check, all eight models were also evaluated by repeated five-fold cross-validation on the full data set. The RQ3 hypothesis model, which is inferential rather than predictive, was fitted to all 223 cases.")
H2("Original Work and Comparison With Current Methods")
P("This study's original contributions are as follows. It defines a governance framework for coding agents that can be tested against field evidence. It produces a three-coder data set of the full population of disclosed coding-agent weaknesses. It audits CVSS records against their own vectors. It formally compares predictors based on governance coding with current weakness-type triage. It measures an LLM coder against human agreement in a way that is not circular. Current practice triages advisories by CVSS score and CWE type, one record at a time. M3 represents that practice, so the gap between M3 and the ACEG models measures what governance coding adds.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Architecture
H1("Architecture Diagram/Workflow")
H2("System Overview")
P("The pipeline turns raw advisory records into three outputs: evidence on the research questions, a trained prediction model, and a scorecard that teams can use for new advisories. It runs in seven stages, from data acquisition to deployment, with a feedback loop from deployment back to the codebook. Each stage is a numbered Python script that reads from and writes to the repository or the SQLite database, so the whole workflow can be rerun from the frozen commits. Every stage runs on a standard central processing unit (CPU); no graphics processing unit (GPU) is required.")
H2("Architecture Diagram")
FIGURE(3, "End-to-End Workflow of the Proposed System", FIG / "fig_architecture.png", 6.3,
       "Colours mark the type of stage (see legend). Solid arrows show data flow from the frozen sources to users; the dashed arrow is the feedback loop through which low-agreement domains and uncovered cases revise the codebook (ACEG Version 2).")
P("As shown in Figure 3, data move from the two Git sources (stage 1) through preprocessing (stage 2) and EDA (stage 3) to feature engineering (stage 4), where the human and LLM coders turn advisory text into governance features stored in SQLite. The models (stage 5) and their evaluation (stage 6) feed the deployment layer (stage 7), which serves the users (stage 8). The feedback loop carries evidence from deployment, such as weak agreement and framework gaps, back into the codebook.")
H2("Workflow Components")
RUNIN("Data Ingestion", "The sources are two Git repositories of JSON advisory records, cloned at fixed commits. src/01_collect.py parses the JSON, filters by publication date and by agent and component names, and writes the 239 candidates to comma-separated values (CSV). src/02_screen.py merges duplicates on the CVE identifier, applies the exclusion codes, and writes the 223 cases. The coders' Excel workbooks and the LLM output are ingested by src/07_parse_llm_codes.py and src/08_build_gold_and_db.py, which load them into SQLite.")
RUNIN("Data Preprocessing", f"*Missing values* were handled according to why they were missing. Of the {C['cvss_missing']} cases without a numeric CVSS score, {C['recovered']} were recomputed from their own version 3.x vectors with the FIRST (2019) formula. All {C['recovered_band_match']} recovered scores match the published severity band. The remaining {C['left_missing']} cases (version 4.0 or no vector) were left missing because the score is not a model input and the outcome uses the published severity, which is complete. The 82 missing titles were filled with the first 120 characters of the description. The two cases without a CVSS vector were excluded only from RQ2, which needs that field. Identifier fields that do not exist in a source (77 GitHub-only and 4 CVE-only identifiers) were left structurally missing. *Outliers* were found with Tukey fences: three CVSS scores below 3.2 and 26 very long descriptions. They were kept because they are valid records rather than errors, and tree models and the bounded GCS are robust to them. *Encoding:* component type, year, and the CVSS version became 0/1 indicators; CWE identifiers were mapped to eight groups and one-hot encoded; control codes were recoded as breach indicators (1 = breached, otherwise 0). *Scaling:* GCS was standardized (z-score) for the logistic model so that its OR is per SD, and the MLP and logistic pipelines standardized all inputs with scikit-learn's StandardScaler, fitted on the training folds only. The tree models need no scaling. A consistency audit found that in {C['score_vector_mismatch']} of the {C['v3_with_score']} cases with a version 3.x vector, the published score differs from the score the vector implies, because the GitHub advisory and the CVE record can carry ratings from different organizations. The severity band differs in {C['band_flip']} cases and the binary outcome in {C['outcome_flip']}. The published severity was kept as the pre-registered outcome.")
RUNIN("Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)", "The EDA (Figures 1, 2, and C1; Table 2) produced descriptive statistics, the distribution of the outcome, product clustering, and χ² screens of each characteristic. Each finding was linked to a modelling decision: imbalance-robust metrics, a single year indicator, product-clustered SEs, and CVSS version as a sensitivity covariate.")
RUNIN("Feature Engineering", "Feature engineering turns text into governance features in three steps. First, three coders produce the domain, control, and coverage codes, which are combined into majority labels. Second, derived features are computed: the GCS = 100 × (applicable − breached) / applicable, the number of domains present, the domain family (A to D) of the primary domain, and 20 breach indicators. Third, covariates and CWE-group dummies are added. CVSS fields are never used as features, to avoid leakage, because they define the outcome.")
RUNIN("Model Development", "The models are the eight described under Materials and Method, from M0 to M7. They were trained on the 75% training split, with hyperparameters tuned by stratified five-fold grid search. The selected settings were: M4 random forest, 500 trees, minimum leaf size 5, √p features per split, balanced class weights; M5 gradient boosting, learning rate 0.03, depth 3, 100 iterations; M6 MLP, two hidden layers of 32 and 16 rectified linear units, L2 penalty 0.001; M7 random forest, 500 trees, maximum depth 5, minimum leaf size 3.")
RUNIN("Model Evaluation", "Every model was scored on the training set, by repeated cross-validation within the training set, and on the untouched test set. The metrics were AUC with a bootstrap CI, accuracy, balanced accuracy, precision, recall, F1 at a threshold of 0.5, and the Brier score. A paired bootstrap compared AUCs, and permutation importance explained the best model.")
RUNIN("Deployment", "The selected model, refitted on all 223 cases, is saved as a model file (models/aceg_risk_model.pkl). It is served by a Flask representational state transfer (REST) API (src/10_scorecard_service.py) with POST /score and GET /scorecard/<case_id> endpoints, and it renders a hypertext markup language (HTML) governance scorecard. The same model, with a dependency-free fallback, is packaged in the ACEG Governance Skill (skill/aceg-governance/; Appendix F), which carries the codebook, blinding, scoring, and remediation steps into any LLM agent environment.")
H2("Tools and Technologies")
P("Table 5 lists the languages, libraries, platforms, and hardware used at each stage. All software is open source except the LLM coder, and the package versions are pinned in requirements.txt so that the results can be reproduced.")
