P(f"Figure 8 explains the result. High-impact cases have a slightly *higher* median GCS ({R3['gcs_median_hi']:.1f}% vs. {R3['gcs_median_lo']:.1f}%), and the unadjusted difference is significant (Mann–Whitney p = {nz(R3['mw_p'],3)}). It reverses the expected direction because GCS is a ratio: a case that implicates more domains brings more applicable controls into the denominator, and many of them hold. The sensitivity analyses (Table D1; Figure D1) confirm that GCS is not a stable predictor. Its OR is about 1.1 and nonsignificant with the Coder 1 or LLM labels, 0.72 (p = .045) with the Coder 2 labels, and 1.12 (p = .784) when the raw count of breached controls replaces GCS, and adding a CVSS-version covariate changes nothing. The substantive answer to RQ3 is therefore about breadth, not depth: what predicts severity is *how many* governance domains fail together and *which* controls fail (Figure 6), not the share of controls that held. A single summary score hides that profile, which is why the profile-based models outperform the GCS model by a wide margin.")
