P("The profile across domains is the most useful part of the result. The LLM reached substantial agreement on the domains with concrete textual cues: secrets and data protection (D5, κ = .74), supply-chain integrity (D6, κ = .63), and input trust (D4, κ = .60). It was weak on the judgement-heavy distinctions: agency versus boundary adherence (D1, κ = .28; D2, κ = .25) and human oversight (D8, κ = .32). Its errors were mostly over-coding. It added D2 in 44 consensus-negative cases, often treating an allowlist bypass as both D1 and D2, and it coded D7 in 21 cases where both humans coded it absent. The low agreement is largely a codebook problem rather than a coder problem. Coder 1 coded transparency (D3) in 57.8% of cases and Coder 2 in none, and the two humans disagreed on the primary domain in 63% of cases (κ = .22). Where the codebook wording is concrete, the LLM is as reliable as a trained human. Where it is ambiguous, neither humans nor the LLM converge. This points directly to the Version 2 changes below.")
H2("Overall Interpretation")
P(f"Taken together, the results show that the ACEG Framework is a valid map of disclosed coding-agent weaknesses: it covers almost every case (RQ1), and its control profile is the strongest available predictor of severity. A random forest on that profile reaches a test AUC of {nz(best.test_auc)}, against .70 for weakness-type triage (RQ3). Three factors explain the performance. First, severity rises with breadth: the number of failing domains more than doubles the odds per domain. Second, a few controls carry most of the signal: privilege scope (C01), automatic execution of repository configuration (C14), untrusted-input isolation (C08), and egress limits (C11). Third, the ratio-based GCS dilutes that signal, so it neither confirms nor refutes H3 and should not be used alone as a risk score. RQ2 shows that human approval is not tied to any one kind of failure, and RQ4 shows that LLM coding is good enough for concrete domains but needs human review for judgement-heavy ones. Small test-set size and modest human agreement mean these estimates have wide intervals, which the next section discusses.")
H2("Practical Significance")
P("The results support four decisions in practice. First, *control priorities:* organizations adopting coding agents should first enforce least-privilege credentials, block automatic execution of repository-level configuration (including Git hooks, MCP definitions, and editor settings), isolate untrusted inputs, and limit network egress. These are the controls whose failure most often goes with high-impact weaknesses. Second, *triage:* security teams can rank incoming agent advisories by the model's predicted probability. At the default threshold this clears about half of the low-impact cases that a CVSS-free rule would escalate, and every probability comes with the specific breached controls. Third, *automation with oversight:* the ACEG Governance Skill packages this triage for industry use; its LLM step can handle first-pass coding of D4, D5, and D6 at about human reliability, while D1, D2, D3, D7, and D8 still need human review. Fourth, *framework revision:* the evidence supports ACEG Version 2 (Table B2). It merges D3 and D7 into one transparency and accountability domain with explicit triggers, adds a decision rule that separates D1 (approval bypass) from D2 (bypass of a configured limit), requires coders to name the control that would have prevented the weakness, and adds two controls, C21 (authenticated, origin-checked local agent endpoints, prompted by 15 cases of exposed hypertext transfer protocol [HTTP] or WebSocket services) and C22 (transport and host verification for remote sessions, prompted by CA-121).")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Implementation
H1("Implementation and User Benefit")
P("The study's outputs are packaged so that a team can use them on the next advisory without re-running the research pipeline. The industry solution is the ACEG Governance Skill: a self-contained skill package that an LLM agent (for example, Claude with Agent Skills, or any agent framework that can read instructions and run Python) loads when a user asks it to assess an AI coding-agent advisory. A Flask scorecard service exposes the same scoring logic to non-agent systems.")
H2("Deployment Approach")
P("The primary deployment is the ACEG Governance Skill (skill/aceg-governance/ in the repository). It follows the open Agent Skills format: a SKILL.md file with a name and a trigger description, so that an agent loads it only when an advisory, CVE, or incident about a coding agent is in scope, plus reference files, scripts, and model assets (Table 11). Figure 10 shows its five-step workflow. Step 1 collects the advisory and removes severity wording with mask_severity.py, the same blinding used in the study. Step 2 has the LLM apply references/codebook.md, which adds the Version 2 decision rules for the low-agreement domains, and return schema-valid JSON with an evidence quote for each domain. Step 3 runs aceg_score.py, which computes the GCS and the predicted probability of high impact. Step 4 enforces the RQ4 review rule by flagging D1, D2, D3, D7, D8 and the primary domain for human confirmation. Step 5 builds a prioritized remediation plan from references/controls_playbook.md, giving each breached control concrete actions, an owner, and a verification check.")
P("The skill is designed to run anywhere. aceg_score.py uses the random forest (M7) when scikit-learn can load it and otherwise falls back automatically to portable_model.json, an L2-penalized logistic model stored as plain coefficients that needs only the Python standard library (cross-validated AUC = .84). Its priority thresholds are calibrated to reproduce the forest's tier shares. The script runs in three modes: a single advisory (JSON and HTML scorecard), a portfolio file (CSV in, scored CSV and tier counts out), and the Flask REST service (src/10_scorecard_service.py), which exposes GET /health, POST /score, and GET /scorecard/<case_id> for ticketing and reporting systems. Nothing requires a GPU, and a case is scored in milliseconds on a single CPU core, so the skill and service can run on an analyst's laptop, inside an agent platform, or as a container on Azure App Service, AWS Lambda, or Kubernetes.")
