P("The skill was validated as an industry user would run it, with the LLM doing all of the coding and no human labels. On the 56 held-out test cases, scoring the LLM coder's labels with the hold-out random forest gave AUC = .81, 95% CI [.68, .92], balanced accuracy = .71, and recall = .90. That is close to the AUC of .84 obtained with the majority labels and well above weakness-type triage (.70). The portable fallback reached AUC = .81 on the same automated path. The priority tiers also held up out of sample: 18 of 20 Priority 1 cases (90%) were rated High or Critical, compared with 17 of 23 Priority 2 cases (74%) and 4 of 13 Priority 3 cases (31%). The tiers therefore separate urgent from routine work (outputs/final/tables/skill_evaluation.csv and skill_tier_validation.csv).")
H2("System Integration")
P("The service fits existing vulnerability-management workflows at three points. In *intake*, a scheduled job subscribes to the GitHub Advisory Database or the CVE feed, filters for the organization's approved agents and extensions, passes each new record to an agent running the ACEG Governance Skill, or calls /score directly with records that are already coded. In *ticketing*, the returned JSON (priority tier, breached controls, OWASP and NIST crosswalk) maps directly to fields in Jira or ServiceNow, so each breached control becomes a remediation subtask with an owner. In *governance reporting*, scorecards accumulate in the SQLite database or a data warehouse and feed the ISO/IEC 42001 management review and the NIST AI RMF measure function with control-level trends. Because inputs and outputs are plain JSON, no change is needed in the agents themselves.")
H2("User Interaction")
P("Users interact through a simple loop. Most users simply ask their agent, for example, “Assess this Cursor advisory with ACEG,” and paste or link the advisory; the skill does the rest. Programmatic users supply a coded record listing the domains present, the primary domain, the breached controls, the CWE identifiers, the component type, and the year. The output is a one-page scorecard (Figure 11) and a remediation plan showing the GCS, the number of domains and the family, the predicted probability of high impact against the portfolio base rate, a priority tier (Priority 1, fix before agent use, at p ≥ .80; Priority 2, fix this sprint, at .50 to .80; Priority 3, routine patch cycle, below .50), the breached controls with their OWASP and NIST mappings, the playbook actions with owners and verification checks, and the codes flagged for human review. Analysts can query the same results through the API or the SQLite views.")
H2("Benefits to Users")
P("*Operational efficiency:* the scorecard replaces manual reading of every agent advisory with a ranked queue. Because the model clears about half of the low-impact cases that a uniform escalation rule would send to the top of the queue, security engineers' effort goes where severity is concentrated, and LLM pre-coding substantially reduces the time spent coding each case. *Financial impact:* prioritizing the few controls linked to severe weaknesses, such as least privilege and blocking automatic configuration execution, lowers the expected cost of agent-driven incidents like the Replit database deletion and avoids spending on controls with little evidence behind them. *Strategic value:* executives and boards get an auditable, framework-aligned view of agent risk across vendors, which supports procurement decisions, ISO/IEC 42001 certification evidence, and the governance commitments regulators and customers increasingly expect.")
H2("Example Use Case")
P("Consider a platform team that has approved Claude Code for its developers. A new advisory appears (CA-169, held out of model training): a sandbox escape through Git worktree path confusion that, when a developer opens a repository containing prompt-injection content, lets the agent overwrite shell start-up files and run code outside the sandbox. The nightly intake agent loads the ACEG Governance Skill, masks the severity wording, codes the advisory, and scores it with the hold-out model. Figure 11 shows the scorecard. Four domains are implicated (agency, boundary adherence, input trust, and supply-chain integrity). Six of ten applicable controls held (GCS = 60%), the predicted probability of high impact is 86%, against a 70% base rate, and the case is placed in Priority 1. The four breached controls, C03 execution isolation, C04 policy limits, C08 untrusted-input isolation, and C14 no auto-execution of workspace configuration, become four tickets, each carrying the playbook action and verification check. For C14, for example, the action is to disable Git hooks and fsmonitor when an agent opens a repository, and the check is that a booby-trapped repository runs nothing. The skill flags D1, D2, and the primary domain for analyst confirmation before the tickets are released. The team pins the patched version, adds a policy that blocks agents from running in repositories that have not been explicitly trusted, and records the action for its ISO/IEC 42001 audit trail. The published CVSS rating, which neither the coders nor the model saw, is High, matching the prediction.")
