# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Limitations
H1("Limitations and Further Improvements")
P("The findings rest on public disclosures, a small number of low-impact cases, and coders who agreed only moderately. These constraints are set out below, together with their effects and the work that would address them.")
H2("Limitations")
P("*Data limitations.* Advisories reflect disclosure behaviour as well as product risk. Vendors with active security programmes, such as Anthropic, Cursor, and Microsoft, publish more advisories, so counts by product are not safety rankings. CVSS measures potential technical impact rather than realized or ethical harm, and the score–vector audit showed that different scoring organizations disagree on 43 of 132 version 3.x records. Almost all cases (99%) date from 2025 onward, and one-sentence CVE records (26 cases under 200 characters) give coders little to work with. *Methodological and model limitations.* The two human coders agreed weakly (pooled κ = .28), and the final labels are majority votes that include the LLM, so the labels carry measurement error. The data are observational, so the results show associations, not causes. With 223 cases and a 56-case test set, AUC intervals are wide (about ±.10), and the differences among the tree-based models are not statistically distinguishable. The MLP overfit despite regularization, and the random forest is less transparent than logistic regression, although permutation importance mitigates this.")
H2("Impact of Limitations")
P("These limitations affect the findings in specific ways. Label noise in D1, D2, D3, D7, and D8 weakens any relationship involving those domains, which biases the RQ2 and RQ3 tests toward the null. The nonsignificant GCS result (p = .057) is therefore weak evidence of no effect, not strong evidence of absence. The coverage and prevalence estimates (RQ1) and the tree-model performance are robust across label sources. Disclosure bias limits generalization to products that publish advisories, and the concentration in 2025 and 2026 means the model reflects the current generation of agents, so its calibration should be rechecked as products and scoring practice change. The RQ4 consensus evaluation leaves out the hardest cases, the ones the humans disagreed on, so it may overstate the LLM's agreement on a fresh sample. In the other direction, the low human benchmark means that κ > .60 may be an unrealistic target until the codebook is revised.")
H2("Future Improvements")
P("Four improvements follow directly. First, apply ACEG Version 2 (Table B2), run a calibration round of 20 to 30 cases with a third adjudicating coder, and recode the full data set. Human κ should rise above .60 before the LLM is benchmarked again. Second, add exploitation evidence, such as the Exploit Prediction Scoring System and known-exploited-vulnerability lists, as alternative outcomes that are less tied to one organization's CVSS judgement. Third, model the four-level severity with ordinal and mixed-effects models that treat product as a random effect. Fourth, give the skill's LLM step few-shot examples from adjudicated cases for the weak domains, and re-validate the skill each quarter against newly disclosed advisories so that its calibration keeps pace with new agent releases.")
H2("Future Scope")
P("The approach extends naturally in several directions. The data set can be refreshed quarterly to track whether governance failures shift as vendors adopt sandboxes and permission systems. The framework can be applied to other agent types, such as browser, data-analysis, and customer-service agents, and to incident reports and internal security reviews beyond public advisories. The ACEG Governance Skill can be published to organizational and public skill catalogues, extended with connectors that pull advisories from vendor feeds automatically, and adapted to other agent classes through new codebook modules. The scorecard can be linked to runtime telemetry so that control breaches are detected in agent logs before a vulnerability is disclosed. The researcher plans to continue this line of work at the doctoral level, testing whether organizations that implement the ACEG priority controls experience fewer and less severe agent incidents.")

